// ---------- Uptime Kuma integration
$add('KUMA-001', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Metrics parser against a sample /metrics payload', 'pass', 'A fixture with six monitors (http, ping, port) parses correctly: statuses 1/0/2/3 map to up/down/pending/maintenance, response time -1 and NaN become empty, certificate days and validity are read, names containing quotes and braces survive, a trailing timestamp is accepted, unrelated metrics are ignored, monitors are sorted with down first, and malformed or empty input never causes an error.');
$add('KUMA-002', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Encryption of the API key at rest', 'pass', 'AES-256-GCM round trip returns the key; the ciphertext never contains it; every encryption differs; tampering or the wrong key is rejected; a missing encryption key fails loudly instead of storing plain text. The existing application key is reused.');
$add('KUMA-003', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Address validation and request safety', 'pass', 'http and https only, no credentials, query or fragment in the address, port range checked. Internal and loopback addresses are allowed because Kuma normally runs internally, while link-local (cloud metadata), unspecified and multicast addresses are refused. The connection is pinned to the validated address, redirects are not followed, the wait is 5 seconds and the response is limited in size.');
$add('KUMA-004', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Settings access and secrecy of the key', 'pass', 'Signed-out requests get 401. A normal user gets 403 on the endpoints and the tab and does not see the tab in the menu, even with other permissions. A missing or wrong CSRF token is 403. The GET response contains exactly four fields and never the key; the page shows only a masked placeholder; the stored value is ciphertext. A blank key keeps the stored ciphertext unchanged; a typed key replaces it; a failed form never echoes a typed key; the log never contains the key.');
$add('KUMA-005', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Test connection outcomes', 'pass', 'Valid key: success with the monitor count. Bad key: unauthorized. HTML answer: invalid response. Server error, closed port and a slow server that exceeds the time limit: unreachable, without passing on upstream error text. Invalid addresses and the metadata address are refused before any request. Testing never changes the saved settings.');
$add('KUMA-006', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Monitoring service: states and caching', 'fixed', 'Not configured, up to date, unauthorized, unreachable and invalid-response states all behave, and the last good data is returned flagged stale instead of a crash. Ten dashboard requests reach Kuma once; failures are cached too so a down Kuma is not hammered; saving settings clears the cache. One defect: the monitor fields were not in the documented order.', 'The fields are now produced in the documented order.');
$add('KUMA-007', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Uptime page loads', 'fixed', 'The automated suite found that the Uptime page crashed with a 500 error, because it used a constant defined in a file it had not loaded.', 'The page now loads the Kuma module. Retested: the page loads and polls only the dashboard backend.');
$add('KUMA-008', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Settings tab and Uptime page in the browser', 'pass', 'Empty state with a button to the settings tab; saving shows a confirmation and a masked key; invalid input shows messages under the right fields; Test connection shows the result inline; summary tiles, down-first table, certificate warnings (expired, expires in 9 days) and the stale banner when Kuma goes down all render; no console errors; no accessibility violations in the automated scan; no horizontal scrolling on a phone.');
$add('KUMA-009', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Automated test suite', 'info', 'tests/run.php (166 checks, no framework) covers the parser, encryption, address validation, the monitoring states and caching, the history and uptime calculations, the settings endpoints, the monitoring endpoints and the saved health-API keys, using a mock Kuma on port 3199. Run it with php tests/run.php while Apache and MariaDB are running. It backs up and restores the saved Kuma settings.');
$add('KUMA-010', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Against a real Uptime Kuma server', 'pass', 'Everything was first tested against a mock that reproduces the documented metrics format and the Basic-auth rule. The owner then ran it against their real Uptime Kuma server and reported that it passed (11 November 2026). This is the owner\'s report; the details of that run were not recorded here.');

// ---------- Uptime page in the style of Kuma, with history
$add('KUMA-011', 'Uptime Kuma', 'History: heartbeats, uptime percentages and events', 'pass', 'Each monitor has a stable id. The first fetch records one heartbeat per monitor; a second fetch within a minute with no change records nothing; a status change is recorded at once and marked important; after a minute every monitor gets a new heartbeat. On synthetic history the 24-hour uptime (3 of 4 counted checks, maintenance excluded) and 30-day uptime (5 of 8) and the 24-hour average response (up checks only) are exact, a monitor with no history shows empty rather than 0 percent, and the list view receives the newest 40 heartbeats oldest-first. Detail and events endpoints require a sign-in, reject malformed or hostile ids, and never contain the key. History is deleted after 30 days.');
$add('KUMA-012', 'Uptime Kuma', 'Kuma-style Uptime page in the browser', 'pass', 'Kuma layout in the dashboard own colours (navy panels, lime for up, coral for down, butter for pending, rounded cards and chip-style filter tabs) instead of Kuma black palette. Monitor list with search and Up/Down/Pending/Maintenance filter tabs, Quick Stats and recent status changes on the dashboard view, and a detail view with the status badge, heartbeat bar, response time, 24-hour and 30-day uptime, certificate expiry, a 24-hour response chart with down periods marked, and an important-heartbeats table. Selecting a monitor is kept in the address, so reload and the back button work. Filtering by Down and searching for sip return the right monitors; no console errors, no content-policy violations, no accessibility violations, and no horizontal scrolling on a phone.');
$add('UI-011', 'Interface', 'LinkedIn button on the creator page', 'pass', 'The creator page has a Connect on LinkedIn button that opens the supplied profile in a new tab with rel noopener noreferrer, and a hidden note for screen readers that it opens in a new tab.');

// ---------- Saved keys
$add('KEYS-001', 'Saved keys', 'Health-API keys can be shown again', 'fixed', 'A key used to be displayed once, at creation, and was gone after a refresh, so a new key had to be made every time. Keys are now stored encrypted as well as hashed. Tested: a new key is encrypted in the database; Show key returns the exact key that incoming calls are checked against and it works against the health API; after a page refresh and after a completely new sign-in the same key is shown again; the full key is never written into the page; responses are not cached; every reveal is logged with the key name only. Permissions: Can manage may show keys, view-only and no-access users get 403 and no button, signed-out is 401, a wrong token is 403. Revoked keys can not be shown and stop working at once. Keys made before this change can not be recovered and say so.', 'Added encrypted storage (key_enc), the Show key, Hide key and Copy buttons (the key hides itself after 30 seconds), and api/api_key_reveal.php.');
$add('KEYS-002', 'Saved keys', 'Saved keys survive a database reset', 'fixed', 'Running bin/setup.php --reset dropped the whole database, so a saved Kuma connection, API keys and endpoints were lost, and a new encryption key would have made any kept secrets unreadable. Tested with a real reset on a backed-up database: the Kuma connection, health-API keys, own endpoints and manual test-log entries were kept, the encryption key was reused so the kept key still decrypted to the original and still matched its hash, and --wipe-all removed everything and created a new encryption key. The database was then restored from the backup.', 'A reset now keeps those four things and the encryption key; --wipe-all deletes them too.');
$add('KEYS-003', 'Saved keys', 'The saved Kuma API key persists across refreshes', 'pass', 'In the browser, after saving a Kuma address and key, three page refreshes in a row still showed the address and the masked key (••••••••WXYZ) with "A key is saved". The key is stored encrypted and is deliberately never sent back to the page, so it can be replaced but not displayed.');
$add('KEYS-004', 'Saved keys', 'Development clean-up removed saved settings', 'info', 'While building and testing, some manual clean-up commands emptied the saved Kuma settings, the Uptime history and the activity log. A Kuma key saved during that time had to be entered again. The automated suite restores saved settings after it runs and only deletes rows it created, and the reset script now keeps connection settings.');

